+# ADR-001: Technology stack for the prototype and the final product
+
+| | |
+|---|---|
+| **Status** | Accepted |
+| **Jira** | TM09-122 — Evaluate technology options (epic TM09-84, Design & Architecture) |
+| **Author** | Tamar Shuster |
+| **Input from** | Tareq Haque (first draft, `docs/TECH-DECISIONS.md` on `tareq/sprint-6-design-docs`), Chris Martinez-Rivas, Chang Chang |
+| **Date** | October 28, 2026 |
+
+## Context
+
+SubletU has two jobs to do, and they pull in different directions.
+
+1. **The prototype** (Sprint 7). It has to put a swipeable listing deck in
+   front of students as fast as possible so we can test the idea. It will be
+   thrown away, so speed matters more than structure.
+2. **The final product** (Sprint 8 onward). It has to implement our class
+   diagram (`Data`, `ListingData`, `PhotoData`, `PricingData`, `RoomDetails`,
+   `ScreenSettings`, `ListingManager`) as real code, run on every teammate's
+   laptop and in CI, and be something we can test and explain in the final
+   demo.
+
+Constraints that apply to both:
+
+- **No paid services.** No API key that needs a credit card or billing account.
+  Four students, one semester, no budget.
+- **Runs on everyone's machine.** We develop on macOS and Windows. Setup should
+  be `npm install` and nothing else: no Docker, no database server.
+- **Testable in CI.** GitHub Actions has to be able to run the whole thing.
+- **The map is core.** Students choose sublets by distance to campus, so the
+  map and the geocoding have to be good, not an afterthought.
+
+## Options considered
+
+### Back end
+
+| Option | Speed to first demo | Fits the class diagram | Cost / lock-in | Local + CI | Verdict |
+|---|---|---|---|---|---|
+| **Hosted BaaS (Supabase)** | Fastest. Auth, Postgres and row-level security are ready on day one | Poor. Logic ends up in the client and in SQL policies, not in our classes | Free tier, but the project lives in someone's account. Tied to one vendor | Needs network and a hosted project. CI must hit a real instance | **Prototype** |
+| Firebase | Fast | Poor. Document store does not match our relational ERD | Free tier, vendor lock-in | Emulator works but adds setup | Rejected |
+| **Own API: Express + SQLite** (TypeScript) | Slower. We write auth, routes and the schema ourselves | Direct. Each diagram class becomes a TypeScript class with `extends` | Free, nothing hosted, no account | One file database. Runs offline, CI gets a fresh temp DB per run | **Final** |
+| Own API: Express + Postgres | Same as above | Direct | Free, but needs a Postgres server | Every teammate and CI needs Postgres running | Rejected (setup cost, no benefit at our scale) |
+| Spring Boot / Django | Slower | Good | Free | Adds a second language and toolchain next to the React front end | Rejected (one language across the stack is simpler) |
+
+SQLite comes from Node's built-in `node:sqlite` module (Node 22.6+), so the
+database needs no native package to compile. This was the main Windows pain
+point we wanted to avoid. Node also strips TypeScript types itself and runs
+the `.ts` files directly, so the server has no build step.
+
+### Front end
+
+| Option | Notes | Verdict |
+|---|---|---|
+| **Single HTML file, React + Babel from a CDN** | No install and no build. Open the file and it runs. Fine for a throwaway, but there's no type checking, no modules, no tests, and everything recompiles in the browser | **Prototype** |
+| **React + Vite** (TypeScript) | Fast dev server, real modules, type checking, code splitting (the map bundle loads only on the Map tab), and the Playwright suite runs against the production build | **Final** |
+| Next.js | Server rendering and routing we don't need. Our app is a single-page app behind a login | Rejected |
+| React Native / Expo | Real native app, but a second build chain and device testing for every feature. A mobile-first web app reaches every phone without an app store | Rejected for this course. Revisit after the semester |
+
+### Map and geocoding
+
+The requirement: **a map provider chosen with no paid API key required.**
+
+| Option | Key / billing | Notes | Verdict |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| Google Maps JavaScript API | API key **and** a billing account with a card | The prototype used it, with the key pasted into the HTML. A key committed to a public repo can be copied and run up charges on the owner's account | Rejected |
+| Mapbox GL JS | Access token and account | Polished, but our usage would be tied to one person's token | Rejected |
+| Leaflet + OpenStreetMap tiles | None | Simple and keyless, but raster-only and less smooth on phones | Fallback |
+| **MapLibre GL + OpenStreetMap tiles** | **None** | Open-source fork of Mapbox GL JS. WebGL, smooth pinch-zoom on phones, and it accepts any tile URL. We can switch to vector tiles later by changing one line (`createOsmStyle()` in `web/src/components/MapCanvas.tsx`) | **Chosen** |
+| **Nominatim** (OSM geocoder) | **None** | Free, but its usage policy allows at most 1 request/second and requires attribution. We serialize requests to 1/sec and cache every result in the `geocache` table, so each address is looked up once | **Chosen** |
+
+## Decision
+
+**Prototype stack (Sprint 7):** a single-file `prototype.html` using React from
+a CDN and Supabase for auth and data. The goal is speed for user testing. It is
+archived at `legacy/prototype.html` and is not built on.
+
+**Final stack (Sprint 8 onward):**
+
+- **Server:** Node 22.6+, TypeScript, Express, SQLite via `node:sqlite`.
+  Runtime dependencies are only `express` and `cors`.
+- **Web:** React 18 + Vite, TypeScript.
+- **Map:** MapLibre GL rendering OpenStreetMap tiles. Geocoding through
+  Nominatim, cached in `geocache`. **No API key, no account, no billing.**
+- **Tests:** Node's built-in test runner for the domain and HTTP layers, and
+  Playwright end-to-end tests in mobile and desktop viewports. All of it runs in
+  GitHub Actions.
+
+## Consequences
+
+**Good**
+
+- Every class in the class diagram is a real class in `server/src/domain/`, so
+  the design and the code can be checked against each other.
+- Setup is `npm install`, then `npm run seed`, then `npm run dev`, on macOS and
+  Windows alike, with no hosted service to share credentials for.
+- No API keys or secrets in the front end, and nothing that can run up a bill.
+- CI runs the whole app, including the database, with nothing external except
+  map tiles.
+
+**Costs we accept**
+
+- We write and maintain our own auth (password hashing, sessions, email
+  verification, Microsoft SSO). Supabase gave us this for free in the
+  prototype.
+- SQLite is a single file on a single server. That's fine for a class project
+  and a demo, but production at scale would mean moving to Postgres. Keeping
+  all SQL in the route and domain layer keeps that move contained.
+- OpenStreetMap's tile servers are not meant for heavy production traffic. If
+  SubletU outgrew a demo, we would point `createOsmStyle()` at a hosted tile
+  provider or self-host tiles.
+- Nominatim's 1 request/second limit makes the first seed slow (about 24
+  seconds for 24 listings). The cache makes every later run instant.
+
+## Follow-ups
+
+- Production architecture decision record (`docs/adr-002-rewrite.md`), owned by
+  Tareq Haque, builds on this ADR.
+- Retire `docs/TECH-DECISIONS.md` on Tareq's branch once this ADR is merged, so
+  the decision lives in one place.
